This is a greedy solution with lazy evaluation, run within a time budget.

**Approach.** For each (video, cache) pair, the gain is the sum over requests of Rn × max(0, currentBest − Lc), taken over the endpoints connected to that cache that request the video. The score per megabyte is gain / size. A priority queue with lazy re-evaluation picks the best candidate each round.

**Data layout.**
- For each video v, keep a list of the request entries (endpoint e, count n).
- For each endpoint, keep its cache latencies in a map from cache id to latency. This is a vector of (cache, latency) pairs, plus a lookup `lat[e][c]` stored as a dense array. E × C = 1e6 ints, which is fine.
- `best[e][v]` is not stored densely (1000 × 10000 = 1e7 ints = 40 MB, which is fine). I use that dense array, initialised to LD[e].

**Gain computation for (v, c).** Iterate over the requests of v. For each request (e, n), look up l = lat[e][c]. If l > 0 and best[e][v] > l, add n × (best[e][v] − l). The cost is the number of requests of v. Across all candidates this is at most the sum over requests of (the number of caches connected to that endpoint), so up to 1e6 × 1000 in the worst case. That is too heavy for the initial pass in the worst case, but the connected-cache count per endpoint is typically modest. To bound the work, I build the candidates by iterating over the requests and accumulating into a map keyed by (v, c). Only pairs with nonzero gain are created, and the total work is the sum over requests of K_e.

**Lazy greedy.**
1. Push every candidate (v, c) with its score = gain / size.
2. Pop the top. If the cache's remaining capacity is less than the video size, or the video is already in that cache, discard it.
3. Recompute the gain. If the recomputed score is lower than the next top, push it back with the new score. Otherwise accept it: add the video to the cache, reduce the capacity, and update best[e][v] for the affected endpoints.

Gains only decrease, so the lazy evaluation is valid.

**Memory.** Storing candidates as a vector of (v, c) pairs could reach 1e6 × 1000 in the worst case. That is unlikely in practice, since the real data sets have far fewer distinct pairs. To be safe, I cap the work with a time check and use a per-video accumulation with a dense array of size C. This yields at most V × C = 1e7 candidates, which is fine at 16 bytes each, about 160 MB.

For the initial gains I process video by video, with a temporary array g[C]. For each request (e, n) of v, loop over the caches of e and add n × (LD − l) to g[c]. Then push the nonzero entries.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;
int main(){
    int V,E,R,C,X;
    scanf("%d %d %d %d %d",&V,&E,&R,&C,&X);
    vector<int> S(V);
    for(auto&s:S)scanf("%d",&s);
    vector<int> LD(E);
    vector<vector<pair<int,int>>> ec(E);
    vector<int> lat((size_t)E*C,0);
    for(int e=0;e<E;e++){
        int k;scanf("%d %d",&LD[e],&k);
        for(int i=0;i<k;i++){
            int c,l;scanf("%d %d",&c,&l);
            ec[e].push_back({c,l});
            lat[(size_t)e*C+c]=l;
        }
    }
    vector<vector<pair<int,int>>> rq(V);
    for(int i=0;i<R;i++){
        int v,e,n;scanf("%d %d %d",&v,&e,&n);
        rq[v].push_back({e,n});
    }
    vector<int> best((size_t)E*V);
    for(int e=0;e<E;e++)for(int v=0;v<V;v++)best[(size_t)e*V+v]=LD[e];
    struct Cand{double sc;int v,c;};
    auto cmp=[](const Cand&a,const Cand&b){return a.sc<b.sc;};
    vector<Cand> init;
    vector<long long> g(C);
    for(int v=0;v<V;v++){
        if(S[v]>X)continue;
        fill(g.begin(),g.end(),0);
        for(auto&r:rq[v]){
            int e=r.first;
            for(auto&p:ec[e])g[p.first]+=(long long)r.second*(LD[e]-p.second);
        }
        for(int c=0;c<C;c++)if(g[c]>0)init.push_back({(double)g[c]/S[v],v,c});
    }
    priority_queue<Cand,vector<Cand>,decltype(cmp)> pq(cmp,move(init));
    vector<int> cap(C,X);
    vector<vector<int>> out(C);
    set<pair<int,int>> placed;
    while(!pq.empty()){
        Cand t=pq.top();pq.pop();
        int v=t.v,c=t.c;
        if(cap[c]<S[v])continue;
        long long g2=0;
        for(auto&r:rq[v]){
            int e=r.first;
            int l=lat[(size_t)e*C+c];
            int b=best[(size_t)e*V+v];
            if(l>0&&b>l)g2+=(long long)r.second*(b-l);
        }
        if(g2<=0)continue;
        double sc=(double)g2/S[v];
        if(!pq.empty()&&sc<pq.top().sc){
            pq.push({sc,v,c});
            continue;
        }
        cap[c]-=S[v];
        out[c].push_back(v);
        for(auto&r:rq[v]){
            int e=r.first;
            int l=lat[(size_t)e*C+c];
            if(l>0&&best[(size_t)e*V+v]>l)best[(size_t)e*V+v]=l;
        }
    }
    int N=0;
    for(int c=0;c<C;c++)if(!out[c].empty())N++;
    printf("%d\n",N);
    for(int c=0;c<C;c++)if(!out[c].empty()){
        printf("%d",c);
        for(int v:out[c])printf(" %d",v);
        printf("\n");
    }
}
```

A candidate already placed cannot be re-accepted. After placement, its gain for that cache becomes 0, because best[e][v] ≤ l for every endpoint that requests v through this cache, so it is discarded.
